# What this repository states is not part of what it is.
#
# One glob per line, matched against the path relative to this root. These are notes about how the
# work is done rather than statements of what the work is for, and reading them is the instrument
# reading its own notes: the vocabulary of evidence, votes, witnesses and abstention is the reader's
# own, and every occurrence of it in a plan or a to-do list is mass the reading would award itself.
#
# README.md is deliberately absent. It states what this library is for, so it is read — and it also
# quotes the reading's own figures, which is the self-reference the backlog measures rather than hides.

BACKLOG.md
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
CHANGELOG.md

# The plan documents. They specify readings that are not built, in the vocabulary of the domains they
# would read, so leaving them in the corpus would put ForeignExchange and InterestRate into the tree
# and then find them.
docs/plans/**

# The skills index and the script that installs one. They state how the work is done.
docs/SKILLS.md
install-*-skill.sh

# The glossary. It defines morphology, semantics, lemma and synset for a reader who is not a linguist,
# and this repository's stated goal is to be placed under computational linguistics by evidence. A
# glossary of the field written into the corpus would satisfy that goal by construction.
docs/GLOSSARY.md

# Surveys of the field. They are notes on what other people have built and written, held here for
# reference, and they name every method and resource computational linguistics has — which is the same
# self-fulfilment the glossary would commit, at the scale of a discipline rather than a word list.
docs/reference/**
OpenAlex/**
